.TH LORIGINTEST 1
.SH NAME
lorigintest \- check that moving a window leaves nothing behind
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lorigintest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lorigintest
moves a window and checks two things about the result: that none of the
window is left at the place it came from, and that whatever was underneath
it is revealed rather than painted over.
Both halves are checked because the obvious fix for the first causes the
second.
.PP
It runs entirely offscreen.
A
.B Screen
is allocated over an ordinary image rather than over the display's own
screen, so
.B readpixels
works \(em on the display's screen it reads back black \(em and nothing
here depends on a window system, on
.IR tk (2),
or on any particular host graphics backend.
.I Image.origin
reaches
.B memlorigin
through the same
.RB ` o '
message
.IR draw (3)
uses for a real window move, so the path under test is the real one.
.PP
Two arrangements are measured.
In the first a single window sits on nothing but the screen's fill, which
is the case where no layer lies behind to be exposed.
In the second a window overlaps one behind it, so part of what it vacates
belongs to another window and part to the background.
.PP
Every arrangement states its premises first \(em that the window really
did cover its own rectangle, that the one behind really did show where the
front one did not reach \(em so that a broken harness reads as a failure
rather than as a pass.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/lorigintest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (2),
.IR draw (3),
.IR tkofftest (1)
.SH BUGS
It covers the
.B libmemlayer
path and nothing downstream of it.
A window move also passes through the host's own upload path \(em on
MacOSX the tile marking in
.B win-cocoa.m
\(em and this never reaches it, so an afterimage that appeared only there
would not be caught here.
.PP
Colours are compared byte by byte, and
.B RGB24
.B readpixels
hands back blue, green, red in that order.
Assuming the other order inverts every check, and did.
